{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261124T194049Z-03e0ff55", "ts": "2026-11-24T19:45:48Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.9.1-dev", "branch": "feature/static-content", "build": "88c92a5b7209"}, "body": {"base_commit": "184570dda5df24231001f2ed12cef2a0f7327c95", "base_tree": "f7bff0f6658142ef7168b48478db8133f555a799", "head_tree": "50219df47d2244a4ec725ecba85d5cae39f68893", "head_commit": "8cad00a08a89d166ebfa2d3547a7334e0d6325e8", "dispatch_commit": "8cad00a08a89d166ebfa2d3547a7334e0d6325e8", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/coverage_algebra.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_bounded_cumulative.py", "tests/test_coverage_algebra.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_plugin_runtime.py", "tests/test_suite_coupling_declaration.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py", "tests/test_verify_coverage_gate.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-static-content.md", ".prawduct/changes/static-content.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/coverage_algebra.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/templates/project-state.yaml", "tests/test_bounded_cumulative.py", "tests/test_coverage_algebra.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_plugin_runtime.py", "tests/test_suite_coupling_declaration.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py", "tests/test_verify_coverage_gate.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-static-content.md", ".prawduct/changes/static-content.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "is_static_content is fed a committed-tree static declaration and a working-tree coupling declaration, so an uncommitted coupling removal relaxes committed-tree gates", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the Chunk 01 fix moved only the static declaration to the judged tree, but the coupling declaration now feeds the same narrowing (`is_static_content`'s coupled_prefixes clause), and it is still read from the working tree. Example: HEAD and the run's tree both declare `static_content_prefixes: [site/]` and `suite_coupled_prefixes: [site/data/]`. An uncommitted edit drops `site/data/` from the coupling list. A committed `site/data/fixture.json` that a test reads then counts as static in three places: `gates._tree_met` when the target is a committed tree (`suite_vouches_for_tree(project_dir, head_tree)` on the PR gate's base-advance transfer, and `_store_run_vouching` with that target) vouches for stale evidence; `gates.verify_coverage` demands no test reference for it; and `critic_consolidate._derive_roster` (cumulative, head_tree committed) leaves it out of the 12-file count. Neither compared tree supports any of those results. This is the defect the Chunk 01 review found ('an uncommitted declaration relaxed a comparison neither tree supported'), reached through the other declaration, against the plan's own rule 'the declaration in force is the one the judged tree carries' (settled by 'authority fails closed'). It was not reachable before this change for judgeable files: the coupling list only ever widened, and `verify_coverage` and the roster never read it. Fix: have one owner read both declarations from the same tree whenever they are passed to `is_static_content` (for example, `evidence.static_declaration_in_tree` returns the coupling list it read beside the static one), or declare nothing static when the working `project-state.yaml` differs from the judged tree's. Then add a red-verified test per member: an uncommitted coupling removal must not relax the comparison. A safe-direction asymmetry is acceptable: an uncommitted ADDED coupling only widens.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A call site that passes a committed-tree static declaration to is_static_content (directly or via affects_test_outcome/is_executable_path/suite_coupled_files) together with suite_coupled_prefixes read from the working tree, while judging a committed tree.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/gates.py:_tree_met (committed target_tree)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/gates.py:verify_coverage (nonexecutable list)"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py:_derive_roster"}], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Plan deliverable unmet: review-cycle.md's Execution row still says '12+ judgeable files change' with no static discount", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Chunk 02's deliverables name 'the roster rule restated in its home, `plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md` (the Execution row and the paragraph under it)'. The paragraph under the table was updated. The Execution row (line 58, the `final` and `cumulative` cells) still reads 'Coordinator when a risk surface is touched or 12+ judgeable files change'. A declared static file is judgeable, so on the case this work adds the row now states the opposite of `_derive_roster`. No mid-build decision records skipping it, though the token-ceiling decision suggests why it was skipped. Core rule 'Verify a chunk against the PLAN's deliverable list, not the files you edited'. Fix: make the row relational ('12+ non-static judgeable files'), or point it at the paragraph below, and pay the tokens in place or raise the ceiling with a reason.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md"], "scope": "instance", "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The static declaration is read from the judged tree, but the coupled declaration that overrides it is still read from the working tree", "recommendation": "Scope: class. It broke because `is_static_content` needs two declarations, the static prefixes and the `suite_coupled_prefixes` that win any overlap, and each caller fetches them separately: the static one from a committed tree (the Chunk 01 review's fix) and the coupled one from the working tree, as before. An uncommitted edit that REMOVES a coupled prefix therefore still lightens a committed comparison. Take a committed tree that declares static `design/` and coupled `design/data/`, with the coupled line dropped in the working tree only. Then (a) the PR gate's `suite_vouches_for_tree` -> `_tree_met` (target `HEAD^{tree}`) treats a changed `design/data/x.json` as static and reads `current`, where before this change it read stale because the file was judgeable; (b) `verify_coverage` asks no test to reference it; (c) `_derive_roster` does not count it. That falsifies `project-state-keys.md`'s \"An uncommitted declaration lightens nothing\" and the plan's own rule that \"the declaration in force is the one the judged tree carries\". Fix it through one owner: have `evidence.static_declaration_in_tree` become a reader that returns both declarations as the tree holds them, the coupled one keeping its fail-closed `(\"\",)` on an unparseable key, and pass that pair at every site. Make `coupled_prefixes` on `is_static_content` and `is_executable_path` required, with no `()` default, so a caller cannot supply the static half without its overrider. Add the falsifying test above: an uncommitted coupled removal must leave the change stale and counted. Search: every call to `is_static_content`, `is_executable_path` or `suite_coupled_files` that is given a tree-read static declaration.", "files": ["plugin/lib/gates.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/coverage_algebra.py"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A call site that passes a static-content declaration read from a committed tree together with suite-coupled prefixes read from the working tree.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/gates.py::_tree_met"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/gates.py::verify_coverage"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py::_derive_roster"}], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "rule-unenforced: statements of the roster's volume escalator outside the edited paragraph still say '12+ judgeable files' with no static discount", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The rule is core.md's \"A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees: search for the claim, not the tokens you edited\", and no check owns it for the roster rule. In `plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md` the `final` and `cumulative` cells of the Execution row (line 58) still say \"Coordinator when a risk surface is touched or 12+ judgeable files change\". Static files are judgeable, so for 15 declared static files the row says three reviewers while `_derive_roster` picks one. The paragraph under the row now contradicts it (framework Check 9, cross-section consistency). The plan lists \"the Execution row and the paragraph under it\" as a Chunk 02 deliverable, and only the paragraph changed. The Critic cell of `.prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md` row 'Risk surfaces (review depth)' says the same: \"a matched surface at any size, or 12+ judgeable files\" (framework Check 10: a registry row is wrong after a coverage change). So does the comment on `COORDINATOR_JUDGEABLE_THRESHOLD` (\"Judgeable-file count at which...\"). Fix the members, then mechanize the class. Either extend `tests/test_bounded_cumulative.py::TestProtocolAgreesWithCode` so every protocol or registry statement of the volume threshold carries the static qualifier, or state the escalator in one place (the paragraph) and point the row and the registry cell at it rather than restating it.", "files": ["plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "check": "rule-unenforced", "scope": "class", "closure": "A statement, in protocol, registry or code-comment prose, of the coordinator's judgeable-file volume threshold that does not exclude declared static content.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md:58 Execution row, final and cumulative cells"}, {"id": "m2", "site": ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md:19 Risk surfaces row, Critic column"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py COORDINATOR_JUDGEABLE_THRESHOLD comment"}], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A misread static_content_prefixes declaration fails silently: no surface says the declaration was refused", "recommendation": "core.static_content_prefixes_from_text folds YAML_UNPARSEABLE into (), and evidence.static_declaration_in_tree folds a git failure into (). From there every consumer reads 'nothing declared': the roster rationale in critic_consolidate._derive_roster, the test-status reason in gates._tree_met, the coverage-floor note in gates.verify_coverage, and evidence.report_predates_tree. The failure direction is safe, since it costs the full path and never a lighter one. But a consumer who writes the natural YAML form `static_content_prefixes: [design-system/]` (tests/test_suite_coupling_declaration.py pins that this form declares nothing) keeps paying the ~25-minute boundary cost #1239 was filed about. Nothing tells them why: the rationale just leaves out the '(N declared static, not counted)' clause. Its sibling in the same rationale does say so: risk.paths_touch_risk_surface reports SOURCE_UNPARSEABLE ('unparseable risk_surfaces'), and core.read_block_sequence's own docstring makes the point ('declared in a shape the reader does not parse (say so; silently degrading past an operator's declaration is the failure mode)'). The hook-surface rule 'Pick each fallback's direction from the invariant it protects: ... degraded advice names its consequence' applies. Fix: have the reader return the status (as risk.py's reader does), and when the key is present but refused, name it in the roster rationale and the test-status reason, e.g. 'static_content_prefixes unreadable (block sequence only) \u2014 nothing treated as static'. Pin that rendered branch with a test. Scope: instance. It broke because the new reader drops the status the shared parser already returns. Its sibling suite_coupled_prefixes also drops it, but there the safe direction is visible as a named stale reason, so it is not in this finding's closure.", "files": ["plugin/lib/core.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "A consumer of the static_content_prefixes declaration that cannot tell 'refused' from 'absent' when it reports its answer.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/core.py:static_content_prefixes_from_text"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py:_derive_roster rationale"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/lib/gates.py:_tree_met reason"}], "fid": "R-5"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 5, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "'An uncommitted declaration lightens nothing' is stronger than the code for a `final` review on a dirty tree", "recommendation": "For `final` (and `chunk`), `begin_review` sets head_tree to `capture[\"tree\"]`, the working tree, so `_derive_roster` reads an uncommitted declaration and does discount by it. Likewise a suite run recorded on a dirty tree carries its uncommitted declaration into later freshness checks. The behaviour fits the plan's rule ('read from the tree it reviews'), and the reviewer sees the declaration in that diff. But the `_derive_roster` docstring and `plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md` state the stronger guarantee unconditionally, and the test pins only the cumulative case. Make the claim relational ('read from the tree under review') rather than absolute.", "files": ["plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "title": "Chunk 02's roster test uses 42 static files, not the plan's 45", "recommendation": "`TestDeclaredStaticContentDoesNotBuyTheCoordinator.STATIC` holds 40 pages plus a stylesheet and a tokens file (42), where the plan's Tests line says 45. The behaviour under test is identical, nothing reads the figure, and no edit is wanted.", "files": ["tests/test_critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "The roster rationale counts declared static files outside 'judgeable'", "recommendation": "`_derive_roster` renders \"0 judgeable file(s) (42 declared static, not counted)\" for 47 changed files, 42 of which are judgeable by `is_judgeable_path`. The plan's architecture keeps static content judgeable. A relational form such as \"N counted judgeable file(s), M declared static not counted\" would avoid implying they are not judgeable. Nothing reads the string except humans and a substring test, so no edit is owed.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Redundant lazy import and import-block churn", "recommendation": "`_derive_roster`'s lazy import now adds `evidence`, which `critic_consolidate.py` already imports at module level (line 84). In `gates.py` the `from .core import` line was reflowed into a parenthesised block with no name added, which is leftover churn. Neither changes behaviour.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/gates.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The plan descopes part of the owner's ruling (no paired native review) on its own reasoning", "recommendation": "Item 1 of the 2026-11-24 ruling in .prawduct/owner-rulings.md describes the lighter review as 'one independent reviewer, no paired native review'. The plan's Tradeoffs put the native-review half out of scope because it runs only on development builds. native_review.py's header confirms that, so the reason is true and recorded. Still, the descoping is the agent's reading of an owner-stated element, and no owner confirmation of it exists. Put the one line to the owner at the PR, or note the confirmation beside the ruling, so the ruling and the shipped behaviour do not appear to disagree.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-static-content.md"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Records Pass covered all three oracle files; none clears a bar", "recommendation": "Rated .prawduct/artifacts/architecture.md, .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-static-content.md and .prawduct/changes/static-content.md against the three bars. The fragment's claims (JUnit ingest, the CAUSE explanation, the coverage floor, the fail-closed reader, the roster reading the reviewed tree, the rationale count) each match the code. One record imprecision clears no bar: Chunk 02's deliverables name review-cycle.md's 'Execution row and the paragraph under it', but only the paragraph changed, and the table row still reads '12+ judgeable files'. The plan is not consumer-facing and nothing acts on that line, so no edit is wanted for the record. Whether the row itself is drift is a Goal 4 question about the subject file.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-static-content.md"], "oid": "O-6"}], "duration_seconds": 420, "scope": "static-content", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_fro